{"name": "contextual_oddity_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "People interpret referring expressions by integrating communicative informativeness with contextual oddity salience. Before hearing an utterance, visual attention is captured by a solitary odd-one-out that breaks the homogeneous background of duplicate distractors, establishing a default referential prior over objects. Pragmatic listeners invert an informative speaker using this oddity-grounded referent prior, defaulting to uniform expectations when multiple distinct items or no oddities exist.", "context": "round 2 candidate 1 explore"}
{"name": "bounded_depth_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions with a strictly bounded reasoning depth, inverting a communicative speaker who reasons about literal semantics rather than engaging in higher-order recursive mental simulation. This bounded depth prevents over-implicature, naturally preserving non-zero probability for multi-feature scalar competitors while strongly favoring minimal referents when words are universally shared. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 2 candidate 2 explore"}
{"name": "graded_truth_listener", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: graded_truth_listener: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "People interpret referring expressions using graded semantic truth values that scale inversely with a referent's feature complexity. When hearing a word, listeners evaluate an object's applicability not as a rigid binary match, but as graded truth that diminishes as the object possesses more competing visual features. On uninformative trials without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 2 candidate 3 explore"}
{"name": "test_model", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: test_model: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Test hypothesis.", "context": "round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent rsa_l2_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 retry 1"}
{"name": "grammatical_exhaustification_distinct", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Refining grammatical_exhaustification by incorporating continuous contextual distinctiveness into the visual attention prior: listeners interpret referring expressions through grammatical exhaustification over unasserted alternative features, ruling out referents proportionally to the informativeness of any more specific descriptions the speaker omitted, while spontaneous visual attention weights referents by combining discrete singleton salience with continuous feature contrast (contextual distinctiveness) relative to display competitors. This directly improves the representation of displays lacking identical duplicates as well as empty singletons in full-duplicate contexts, addressing the empirical discrepancy where humans allocate substantial prior belief to empty singleton objects among identical full-feature duplicates. Differences from grammatical_exhaustification: recursion depth remains unchanged (depth 0, L_exh); parameter w_distinct is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed; and the visual prior is extended by adding w_distinct times the total contextual feature contrast (summed Hamming distance across all context objects).", "context": "round 2 candidate 6 refine chosen"}
{"name": "graded_truth_listener_2", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: graded_truth_listener_2: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "People interpret referring expressions using graded semantic truth values that scale inversely with a referent's feature complexity. When hearing a word, listeners evaluate an object's applicability not as a rigid binary match, but as graded truth that diminishes as the object possesses more competing visual features. On uninformative trials without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 2 candidate 3 explore repair 1"}
{"name": "rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "RSA pragmatic listener at depth 1 with singleton salience, contextual distinctiveness, visual feature complexity, evaluative valence framing, visual color contrast, and literal L0. Refining rsa_l2_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 by reducing pragmatic recursion depth from depth 2 to depth 1: listeners interpret referring expressions by inverting an informative speaker who evaluates literal semantics, rather than engaging in higher-order recursive mental simulation. This bounded depth eliminates over-implicature in scalar implicature and prevents multiplicative prior compounding across reasoning tiers, preserving appropriate probability on multi-feature competitors while maintaining common knowledge of visual singleton salience, continuous contextual distinctiveness, valence-modulated feature complexity, and color contrast. Differences from rsa_l2_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0: recursion depth is reduced from depth 2 to depth 1 (listener calls L1 instead of L2), the depth-2 listener memo L2 is removed, and all parameters (alpha, w_singleton, w_distinct, w_features, w_valence, w_color) are preserved without addition or removal.", "context": "round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent rsa_l2_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 retry 1 repair 1"}
{"name": "slot_6", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "the agent wrote no candidate.py", "hypothesis": "", "context": "round 3 candidate 6 refine chosen retry 1"}

